              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Krista T"
            },
            "reviewBody": "These are grreat cookies! I did substitute 2tsp cinnamon, 1tsp ground cloves, and, 1/2 tsp ground ginger instead of pumpkin spice. Turned out wonderful!"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Pam Ziegler Lutz"
            },
            "reviewBody": "These have a great pumpkin flavor and the raisins and walnuts are a wondeful addition. The cinnamon glaze makes these even better. Please keep in mind that the description specifically states that these are cakelike cookies. They are not supposed to spread out, but rather stay in a mound when baked. The only problem I ran into was the baking time. I had one tray of cookies that appeared done (after 14 minutes), but when cooled were still a little too gooey inside. I may try baking at 375 F next time. Thanks for a great recipe!",
            "datePublished": "2010-11-13T03:21:05.747Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Rex Fermier"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Very tasty cookies! This recipe is missing, what I consider, important information. Specifically,, how big should I make the cookies? The term \"heaping spoonfuls\" is ambiguous as the size if the cookie will affect the baking time. I used a #50 scoop to make the cookies and baked them until they were a light brown along the edge. About 14-15 minutes. It yielded 40 cookies.\nAgain, these cookies are very tasty!",
            "datePublished": "2019-10-16T19:39:14.660Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Catherine"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Omg so good. Others have said “very cakey”. Yup, that they are. I made them gluten free using Bobs Red Mill 1-1 flour and 1/8tsp xanthum gum, let them sit in fridge a couple days (it’s a gf thing) then baked @16 minutes. Checked at 5 and they weren’t flattening so I squished them a bit with my hand. Great flavor. Used pecans cause couldn’t find my walnuts. It’s a keeper! Oh, Had to make my own pumpkin pie spice mix too but that’s easy :)",
            "datePublished": "2025-09-15T22:48:31.383Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Gail Early"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Pumpkin is very expensive where I live in Mexico and I have substituted baked sweet potatoes or pureed baked squash for the pumpkin. The best result was with the sweet potatoes but there were no complaints about the cookies made with the squash. One trick I learned for making cookies that contain raisins is to beat the eggs, add them to the raisins and let them sit for at least an hour, stirring occasionally. Then add to the batter when the eggs would be added. ...this takes the taste and texture of any cookie up a notch. I am getting ready to make another batch and since I have run out of pumpkin pie spice I will add the other spices as suggested in a previous review, plus allspice. My expat friends love these cookies!",
            "datePublished": "2015-12-23T04:14:04.833Z"
          },
          {
